<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach</h1><p class="st-meta">1741 CE – 1918 CE · capital Weimar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Thuringian duchy whose court at Weimar drew Goethe, Schiller, Herder and Wieland and became the heart of German classicism.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> and Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> were united in 1741 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-i-duke-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Duke Ernest Augustus</a> I of Saxe-Weimar inherited Eisenach on the extinction of its ducal line, creating Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Duchess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-amalia-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Anna Amalia</a>, regent from 1758, invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-martin-wieland">Christoph Martin Wieland</a> as tutor; her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-augustus-grand-duke-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Charles Augustus</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Goethe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1775, later followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller">Schiller</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> flourished. Raised to a grand duchy in 1815, it adopted one of the first German constitutions in 1816. Later grand dukes hosted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> and founded the Weimar art school.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The grand duchy joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871. Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ernest-grand-duke-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">William Ernest</a> abdicated in November 1918, and in 1920 the territory joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-thuringia">Free State of Thuringia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> as capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Gro%C3%9Fherzogtum_Sachsen-Weimar-Eisenach_(1813-1897).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1741 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Weimar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Anna Amalia, Charles Augustus, Charles Alexander</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Grand Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach, Sachsen-Weimar-Eisenach</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1830&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4937 410 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5257 666L5257 666M5257 666L5235 666M5257 666L5229 694M5257 666L5199 691M5257 666L5199 691M5257 666L5300 622M5257 666L5250 665"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5257 666h0M5235 666h0M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0M5250 665h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5257 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5257 666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Weimar. <a href="/atlas?year=1830&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
